      {
        "finding_id": "section-a23482742e6e6675ef5b",
        "file": "ournavybarbaryco00alle_ournavybarbaryco00alle_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Our navy and the Barbary corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/ournavybarbaryco00alle",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": [
            "Allen, Gardner Weld, 1856-1944"
          ],
          "date": [
            "1905-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e15e3a557e79edec2ea71b2b5ccd1015a5ec882a666c35aba27115b05eadb074",
        "evidence_excerpt": "The  narrative  of  John  Foss,  seaman  on  the  brig \nPolly  of  Newburyport,  bound  for  Cadiz,  is  interesting. \nOctober  25,  1793,  the  Polly  was  overhauled  by  a \nbrig  flying  the  British  flag.  \"  When  she  came  near \nenough  to  make  us  hear,  she  hailed  us  in  English, \nasked  from  whence  we  came  and  were  bound,  which \nwas  immediately  answered  by  Captain  Bayley.  The \nman  who  hailed  us  was  dressed  in  the  Christian  habit \nand  he  was  the  only  person  we  could  yet  see  on  her \ndeck.  By  this  time  the  Brig  was  under  our  stern  ;  we \nthen  saw  several  men  jump  upon  her  poop  to  hall  aft \nthe  main  sheet  and  saw  by  their  dress  and  their  long \nbeard  that  they  were  Moors  or  Algerines.  Our  feel- \nings at  this  unwelcome  sight  are  more  easily  imagined \nthan  described.  She  then  hove  too  under  our  lee,  when \nwe  heard  a  most  terrible  shouting,  clapping  of  hands, \nhuzzaing,  &c.,  and  saw  a  great  number  of  men  rise  up \nwith  their  heads  above  the  gunnel,  drest  in  the  Turk- \nish habit  like  them  we  saw  on  the  poop.  They  im- \nmediately hoisted  out  a  large  launch  and  about  one \nhundred  of  the  Pirates  jumped  on  board,  all  armed, \nsome  with  Scimitres  and  Pistols,  others  with  pikes, \nspears,  lances,  knives,  &c.   They  manned  about  20",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
        "name_basis": "THEMATIC_CONCEPT",
        "locator": {
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/ournavybarbaryco00alle",
