      {
        "finding_id": "section-f24bd98191fe8ce11454",
        "file": "ournavybarbaryco00alle_ournavybarbaryco00alle_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Our navy and the Barbary corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/ournavybarbaryco00alle",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": [
            "Allen, Gardner Weld, 1856-1944"
          ],
          "date": [
            "1905-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e15e3a557e79edec2ea71b2b5ccd1015a5ec882a666c35aba27115b05eadb074",
        "evidence_excerpt": "Late  in  November,  1800,  the  American  ship  Anna \nMaria  arrived  with  another  invoice  of  naval  stores. \nThe  bey  found  fault  with  some  of  these,  and  Eaton \nsays  in  his  report :  \"  I  believe  the  facts  to  be,  the \ngovernment  are  dissatisfied  that  anything  has  come \nforward.  If  this  opinion  require  evidence,  I  consider \nit  sufficient  to  state  that  the  United  States  are  the \nonly  nation  which  have,  at  this  moment,  a  rich,  un- \nguarded commerce  in  the  Mediterranean  ;  and  that \nthe  Barbary  Regencies  are  Pirates.''^  ^  The  sapitapa \ndemanded  the  Anna  Maria  for  the  service  of  the  gov- \nernment, but  Eaton  refused,  reminding  him  of  the \npledge  he  had  previously  given.  Then  the  sapitapa \noffered  a  freight  of  four  thousand  dollars  for  a  voyage \nto  Marseilles  with  a  cargo  of  oil,  which  was  accepted, \nas  it  involved  no  concession  of  principle.  Later,  how- \never, he  broke  his  contract  and  insisted  on  sending \nthe  vessel  to  London.   To  avoid  this  and  \"  in  order  to",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
