      {
        "finding_id": "section-7a8f97d96c02a0e39e88",
        "file": "ournavybarbaryco00alle_ournavybarbaryco00alle_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Our navy and the Barbary corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/ournavybarbaryco00alle",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": [
            "Allen, Gardner Weld, 1856-1944"
          ],
          "date": [
            "1905-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e15e3a557e79edec2ea71b2b5ccd1015a5ec882a666c35aba27115b05eadb074",
        "evidence_excerpt": "In  the  spring  of  1801  Eaton  had  a  plan  for  a  com- \nmercial convention  between  the  United  States  and \nTunis  which  should  promote  the  business  interests  of \nboth  and  thereby  make  peace  more  firm  and  lasting, \nand  at  the  same  time  more  honorable,  as  he  hoped  to \nget  the  treaty  still  further  amended  in  favor  of  the \nUnited  States.  The  bey  at  first  seemed  favorably  dis- \nposed, but  suddenly  dropped  the  subject.  This  was \ndue  to  the  action  of  France,  which  not  only  seized  and \ndisarmed  two  of  his  corsairs,  but  exercised  protection \nover  all  the  other  powers  against  which  he  could  cruise. \nHe  therefore  once  more  began  to  turn  his  eyes  towards \nAmerica  with  hostile  intent,  and  projects  of  peaceful \ntrade  no  longer  interested  him.^  The  unsatisfactory \narticles  of  the  treaty  which  kept  American  commerce \naway  from  Tunis  or  were  otherwise  objectionable \nwere  finally  amended  by  a  convention  in  1824.^",
        "years_in_scope": [],
        "date_basis": "EXPLICIT_PARAGRAPH",
        "date_evidence": null,
        "matched_names": [],
