      {
        "finding_id": "section-ff5dffea9179f28bfca9",
        "file": "ournavybarbaryco00alle_ournavybarbaryco00alle_djvu.txt",
        "title": "Our navy and the Barbary corsairs",
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/ournavybarbaryco00alle",
        "source_bibliography": {
          "creator": [
            "Allen, Gardner Weld, 1856-1944"
          ],
          "date": [
            "1905-01-01T00:00:00Z"
          ],
          "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
          "evidence_level": null,
          "rights": null
        },
        "source_text_sha256": "e15e3a557e79edec2ea71b2b5ccd1015a5ec882a666c35aba27115b05eadb074",
        "evidence_excerpt": "May  10,  1801,  the  pasha  declared  war  against  the \nUnited  States,  cut  down  the  American  flag-staff  on \nthe  14th,  and  Cathcart  left  Tripoli  for  Leghorn  on  the \n24th,  leaving  the  interests  of  the  United  States  in \nthe  care  of  Nicholas  Nissen,  the  Danish  consul.  On \nhis  way  to  Leghorn  Cathcart  was  held  up  and  plun- \ndered by  a  Tunisian  corsair,  for  which  Eaton  demanded \nsatisfaction  of  the  bey  of  Tunis,  and  it  was  promised. \nThe  pasha  of  Tripoli  sent  out  his  corsairs  at  once  in \nsearch  of  American  prizes,  but  apparently  without \nsuccess,^  as  the  timely  warning  which  had  been  sent \nout  had  put  the  merchantmen  on  their  guard.  The \ntwo  largest  Tripolitan  cruisers,  under  the  command  of \nMurad  Reis,  a  ship  of  twenty-six  guns  and  two  hun- \ndred and  sixty  men,  called  the  Meshuda,  and  a  brig  of",
        "years_in_scope": [],
