      "candidate_id": "travellers-event-0003",
      "lane": "travellers",
      "source_file": "2020-tr-of-sale-et-ses-corsaires-1666-1727-maziane-2009_2020_Tr_of_Sal_et_ses_corsaires_1666-1727_Maziane_2009__djvu.txt",
      "source_type": "OCR_CONTEXT",
      "evidence_state": "ACQUIRED",
      "title": "English translation of and commentary on \"Salé Et Ses Corsaires 1666 -1727\"",
      "url": "https://archive.org/details/2020-tr-of-sale-et-ses-corsaires-1666-1727-maziane-2009",
      "identifier": null,
      "repository": null,
      "acquired_text_excerpt": "The  complicity  which  Sale  constructed  with  the  other  ports  of  the  littoral  extended \nbeyond  the  borders.  The  corso  was  within  a  context  of  an  Entente  Cordiale  between  the \ncorsairs  of  the  Barbary  Coast  of  the  Maghreb.  The  Barbary  alliance  was  self-imposed, \nand  Algiers  was  the  port  which  associated  itself  with  the  most  enthusiasm  in  the  Salentin \nenterprise.  Despite  some  local  resistance,  the  similar  activities  brought  the  different \ncorsair  places  together.  In  a  letter  addressed  to  Seignelay  on  the  1st  of  November,  1683, \nPierre  de  Catalan  complained  that  the  Moroccan  ports  constantly  serve  as  bases  for  the \nAlgerian  corsairs.  Saint- Amans,  for  its  part,  calls  into  question  the  complicity  of  the \ngovernors,  who  endeavor  to  remain  on  good  terms  with  the  Algerians:",
